1
00:00:01,120 --> 00:00:08,720
Welcome to the Subclub Podcast, a show dedicated to the best practices for building and growing app businesses.

2
00:00:09,920 --> 00:00:19,280
We sit down with the entrepreneurs, investors and builders behind the most successful apps in the world to learn from their successes and failures.

3
00:00:20,480 --> 00:00:22,480
Subclub is brought to you by RevenueCat.

4
00:00:23,280 --> 00:00:32,720
Thousands of the world's best apps trust RevenueCat to power in app purchases, manage customers, and grow revenue across iOS, Android, and the web.

5
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:38,240
You can learn more at revenuecat dot com dot Let's get into the show.

6
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:48,720
Hello, I'm your host, David Barnard, and my guest today is Thomas Petit, an independent consultant focused on subscription app growth.

7
00:00:49,040 --> 00:00:55,680
Over the past decade, Thomas has worked with hundreds of clients and helped manage tens of millions of dollars in ad spend.

8
00:00:56,080 --> 00:01:08,880
On the podcast, I talk with Thomas about setting sensible goals for paid marketing, how to measure and learn from the results, and why a single ad creative can completely change the trajectory of a company.

9
00:01:09,520 --> 00:01:12,240
Hey, Thomas, thanks so much for joining me on the podcast today.

10
00:01:12,800 --> 00:01:16,240
It's a second or third time on, but I'm super excited to chat.

11
00:01:16,400 --> 00:01:17,440
We have a lot to talk about.

12
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:18,640
Yeah, thank you very much.

13
00:01:18,880 --> 00:01:19,840
I'm really happy to come back.

14
00:01:20,080 --> 00:01:24,880
I'm a huge fan of the other episodes and I have to say you have really a great diversity of guests.

15
00:01:25,120 --> 00:01:29,360
So I hope I can keep the bar really high, but I'm really happy to come back for a new topic today.

16
00:01:29,840 --> 00:01:36,480
The reason I wanted to have you back on one of your past episodes was amazing, but we talked about very timely things.

17
00:01:36,640 --> 00:01:42,480
We talked very specifically about ATT and we didn't talk as much about general principles around growth.

18
00:01:42,800 --> 00:01:48,880
But the main focus of what I want to get at today is that I've been talking to so many developers struggling with paid UA.

19
00:01:49,280 --> 00:01:50,880
The funny thing is this is not a new thing, right?

20
00:01:51,120 --> 00:02:06,480
Pay UA has always been hard and there's a little bit of romanticism of like, pre ATT it was so easy and that's not true, but it's just a thing that so many apps struggle with, any marketing struggle with, like, how do I make the unit economics work?

21
00:02:06,800 --> 00:02:18,320
And so that's what I wanted to kick off with today, since you talk to so many developers like I do, but then you actually work as a consultant with a lot of them to like, okay, how do we actually make it work?

22
00:02:19,280 --> 00:02:22,480
I don't have to work that side of the equation personally.

23
00:02:22,720 --> 00:02:28,640
So I love talking to someone like you who is day one in, day out with multiple apps, your job is to help them make it work.

24
00:02:29,040 --> 00:02:48,240
I wanted to kick it off with your thoughts around the kind of the minimal viable status, minimal viable app, minimal viable company, whatever that context around, when you should start thinking about paid UA, like what is it takes to actually start making it work?

25
00:02:48,960 --> 00:03:13,920
Yeah, that's a tough question, but I think it's good to ask the question, to be honest, especially in the early stages, like when you really go from zero to one and you don't like, have a bunch of data behind about how it's going and so on, like when you start're just starting, I see too many people like just straight jumping in and I want to spend this money on Facebook or whatever without having done a bit of a health check of why I'm doing it, what I'm going to try to achieve, what stack does it take?

26
00:03:14,240 --> 00:03:22,320
And a lot of it comes around goals and you have to dig and but why, but why again and again, because usually they just tell you, I want to accelerate.

27
00:03:22,560 --> 00:03:23,280
I want more revenue.

28
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:24,240
I want to accelerate.

29
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:25,920
But why can't you do it otherwise?

30
00:03:26,160 --> 00:03:27,920
Or why do you think paid is the solution?

31
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:31,200
And most importantly, like what are your minimum bar on this?

32
00:03:31,520 --> 00:03:32,720
Especially minimum bar of return.

33
00:03:33,040 --> 00:03:36,720
Like what are you expecting from paid for every dollar you put?

34
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:40,320
out there, are you expecting to make three, five, half of one?

35
00:03:40,640 --> 00:03:43,360
And all these situations can justify themselves in cases.

36
00:03:43,760 --> 00:03:48,320
When you have that clear, you're going to avoid a lot of the bad surprises.

37
00:03:48,960 --> 00:03:50,400
Sometimes it will lead to dead end.

38
00:03:50,640 --> 00:03:54,160
I have one app I work with and I've been working with them for quite a while.

39
00:03:54,640 --> 00:03:57,600
I was chatting with them for years before we started working together.

40
00:03:57,840 --> 00:03:58,560
And they come very clear.

41
00:03:58,800 --> 00:04:04,800
They say, if anything can't run at least two weeks' return direct from the money we put there, we're not running it.

42
00:04:05,760 --> 00:04:11,680
And I say, yep, but given the metrics you have, it's going to shut down a lot of options and channels and so on.

43
00:04:11,680 --> 00:04:12,240
I say, yep.

44
00:04:12,400 --> 00:04:16,320
Yeah, but this is what we're willing to accept and anything below that is trash.

45
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:18,320
I say, okay, I mean, that's how you take it.

46
00:04:18,640 --> 00:04:25,200
And on the one channel I'm helping them with, we do manage to reach that goal, but only up to a certain scale.

47
00:04:25,680 --> 00:04:26,640
And there's no more.

48
00:04:26,720 --> 00:04:27,840
I think it's Apple Search Ads.

49
00:04:28,640 --> 00:04:35,680
In this case, it's Apple Search Ads, but like, yeah, can also happen elsewhere, particularly on Android for subscriptions, like even on other channels.

50
00:04:35,840 --> 00:04:38,800
I have the same problem with Google Ads for another client.

51
00:04:39,360 --> 00:04:42,000
It's good to set the bar, like what are you trying to achieve?

52
00:04:42,240 --> 00:04:48,480
And in some cases, you start to understand how aggressive people can go, like in some cases, accelerating.

53
00:04:48,480 --> 00:05:10,480
Accelerating fast is actually a priority because there is a first move advantage or market share matters a lot or there is a network effect of I work with a developer who was putting money on the table that they were not going to recoup directly, but they needed a critical mass of users for the app to have enough value so that then it started creating that virtuous loop.

54
00:05:11,120 --> 00:05:12,320
And they had their goal really clear.

55
00:05:12,560 --> 00:05:15,760
But I think a lot of people go from zero to one, they're just like, oh, I want to accelerate.

56
00:05:15,840 --> 00:05:16,160
That's it.

57
00:05:16,320 --> 00:05:16,880
I want more revenue.

58
00:05:16,960 --> 00:05:18,560
I say, what about what are you expecting?

59
00:05:18,640 --> 00:05:19,760
So I think that's really one.

60
00:05:20,320 --> 00:05:29,600
Very often it's about return goals, but in some cases it's about achieving velocity and network effects, critical mass or market share or on your marketplace.

61
00:05:29,760 --> 00:05:31,920
It could be, I want to rebalance what I have.

62
00:05:32,400 --> 00:05:40,640
I had one case of a marketplace where they had a ton of seller for one category but no buyer and they had a ton of buyer for another category but no sellers.

63
00:05:41,280 --> 00:05:56,640
And so we run acquisitions specifically aimed at rebalancing a little bit these categories where on the surface it looked like it was expensive, but eventually it enabled the other users not to leave to avoid loss.

64
00:05:56,720 --> 00:06:00,480
Basically, basically know your expectations and know what you're after.

65
00:06:01,040 --> 00:06:05,360
And I think a lot of people, they don't ask this question in a deep enough way.

66
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:06,160
Yeah.

67
00:06:06,720 --> 00:06:08,880
Or they give too simplistic of a answer.

68
00:06:09,200 --> 00:06:18,000
I think if you ask most people, the answer would be, I want to spend a dollar and get a dollar twenty back within thirty days or whatever that answer might be.

69
00:06:18,160 --> 00:06:20,320
And, and that's the expectations going into it.

70
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,960
But I think that's already very specific.

71
00:06:23,840 --> 00:06:25,120
That's pretty good to know at that point.

72
00:06:25,280 --> 00:06:32,320
I mean, when I hear that, I'm like, okay, at least these guys know what they can afford and has considered the time frame and is looking at direct.

73
00:06:32,720 --> 00:06:35,680
There are already a lot of sub questions that are answered in the way you frame it.

74
00:06:35,840 --> 00:06:39,440
So I would qualify this as a fairly advanced way to define the goal.

75
00:06:40,000 --> 00:06:45,680
I think it's an advanced way to define the goal, but for most apps, it's a very unrealistic way to define the goal.

76
00:06:45,760 --> 00:06:49,440
And that was the point I was going to get at, is that it's easy to say that, right?

77
00:06:49,680 --> 00:07:00,160
It's easy to think, Okay, my goal is to get a return on ad spend, but when you only have, let's say, a thousand people who have ever downloaded your app, you're very early.

78
00:07:00,960 --> 00:07:06,560
It's just very unrealistic with a thousand ever downloads that you're going to start doing paid UA.

79
00:07:06,800 --> 00:07:11,040
And this is what I hear from people like, Oh, we're just going to turn on marketing and like, we're going to make money.

80
00:07:11,280 --> 00:07:11,920
It's like, no.

81
00:07:12,480 --> 00:07:24,160
Like the very first step is that your first goal should probably be to get to a certain floor of monthly active users where you understand if you even have product market fit.

82
00:07:24,320 --> 00:07:30,800
Like with a thousand people, you don't actually know what your unit economics are with a thousand downloads.

83
00:07:30,960 --> 00:07:33,200
You don't actually know if your onboarding is working.

84
00:07:33,520 --> 00:07:38,400
You don't actually know if you're even in the ballpark of what you should price the app at.

85
00:07:39,120 --> 00:07:58,720
There's so much context behind the goals that if you're really early stage, the goal maybe is just we know we're going to lose money, but we're going to lose money money doing paid UA in the service of experimentation, dialing product market fit, understanding our audience, just getting live, warm bodies into the app and see how it goes.

86
00:07:59,280 --> 00:08:03,760
Because if your goal is to get a return on ad spin as an early stage app, that's probably not going to happen.

87
00:08:04,240 --> 00:08:06,640
And then I think there are other contextual goals as well.

88
00:08:06,640 --> 00:08:16,400
If you're a VC backed company and you need a big outcome in the long run, that's a very different goal than if you're bootstrapped.

89
00:08:16,560 --> 00:08:20,000
Like I was talking to a guy who has amazing ASO.

90
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:24,000
He wants to grow his business, but he can do it in a more methodical way.

91
00:08:24,160 --> 00:08:24,880
He's very profitable.

92
00:08:24,880 --> 00:08:36,000
He built a model that I can spend on paid UA if my return on that investment is relatively confident at 366 days.

93
00:08:36,800 --> 00:08:41,280
So he takes all the initial conversions and then discounts the renewals.

94
00:08:41,520 --> 00:08:45,840
You know, he knows whatever 40% of my annuals are going to renew at year one.

95
00:08:46,240 --> 00:09:07,920
So if you take the revenue on my seven day free trial conversion and you take 40% assumption of one year renewal, as long as I'm profitable at day 366, I can cash flow a certain amount of paid UA and then that 40% is going to renew the next year at 60 or 70% and then the next year after that.

96
00:09:08,160 --> 00:09:15,680
And so he's going to build this bigger business over the long haul by having a goal that doesn't sound amazing.

97
00:09:15,840 --> 00:09:21,520
It sounds scary to a lot of folks on paper, but the context of his app that can work.

98
00:09:22,320 --> 00:09:42,560
So I think you're right that anybody thinking about paid UA needs to be more introspective and realistic about what they are going to be able to achieve with paid UA versus just thinking, I'm going to start spending money and generating a profit on this and getting a direct return on ad spend.

99
00:09:43,120 --> 00:09:48,240
In some cases, you might want to start your A very early and not even build a base before.

100
00:09:48,720 --> 00:09:58,320
Maybe you know that's going to be the channel and you want to know from the very beginning where you are, or you even want to iterate on the product from this kind of traffic.

101
00:09:58,480 --> 00:10:05,680
And that actually happened to me once, almost like the first app where I got the whole budget for myself was really, okay, what are the goals?

102
00:10:05,840 --> 00:10:15,440
And they're like, we need to have at least five hundred new users per day to iterate on this onboarding on this payroll and get retention data that is we're not going to achieve it organically.

103
00:10:15,680 --> 00:10:17,680
We don't have the time to build the organic.

104
00:10:17,760 --> 00:10:19,920
We're going to do it in parallel, but we need to accelerate.

105
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:28,240
We can afford to lose that amount of money, but we need this amount of users and we need to learn within three months how much is coming back.

106
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:31,680
And we basically need to measure all of this to prepare for what was coming next.

107
00:10:32,160 --> 00:10:37,920
I think here it was clearly a VC back startup, but they knew from the start that this is how they would grow.

108
00:10:38,160 --> 00:10:46,400
And so for them, it did make sense to start early, but not with a I'm going to return tweaks on that, but rather to like, let's build the app towards that.

109
00:10:46,960 --> 00:10:48,640
Let's build the foundation and let's also learn.

110
00:10:49,040 --> 00:10:54,000
in the process, not just to iterate on product, but let's learn as early as possible what messaging is working.

111
00:10:54,240 --> 00:11:02,720
This is what we're going to put in the store, what kind of creative is working because we're going to build the team and all the infrastructure to buy in terms of also metrics and tools.

112
00:11:02,960 --> 00:11:11,760
So it was really like a learning goal, like bring the user, but also within three months, we need to have all of this list checked because we want to accelerate.

113
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:12,960
And this is the goal in this case.

114
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:14,400
So there are many kinds of goals.

115
00:11:14,640 --> 00:11:17,280
I think there's another check that really needs to be done.

116
00:11:17,600 --> 00:11:22,880
I call it cash availability is what can you really afford because return is never guaranteed.

117
00:11:23,040 --> 00:11:25,120
Like sometimes it's a bit of of a roller coaster.

118
00:11:25,280 --> 00:11:25,840
You don't really know.

119
00:11:26,000 --> 00:11:28,560
You open a new channel, you scale up, you start from zero.

120
00:11:29,360 --> 00:11:30,960
Even when you're stable, it changes.

121
00:11:31,200 --> 00:11:33,840
So don't assume that you're going to be at this goal.

122
00:11:34,000 --> 00:11:34,880
There might be some loss.

123
00:11:35,120 --> 00:11:38,640
Maybe there's a time of learning period for the algorithm as well, and so on.

124
00:11:39,040 --> 00:11:41,600
And so you need to check a little bit what's the cash availability.

125
00:11:41,840 --> 00:11:52,800
In the case of the example you gave about the developer who could afford to have like over a year of payback on the renewal, he also knew that he had enough cash in the bank to finance this acquisition before the money was coming back.

126
00:11:53,200 --> 00:11:55,760
So that's something that's quite important to know.

127
00:11:56,000 --> 00:12:00,560
If I had to frame it in a bit of a trivial way, I'd say only gamble money you can afford to lose.

128
00:12:01,040 --> 00:12:02,640
Maybe it's going to come back, but maybe not.

129
00:12:02,880 --> 00:12:06,160
And even if it comes back, it may not come back in a week.

130
00:12:06,560 --> 00:12:08,960
So can you actually afford to finance this?

131
00:12:09,440 --> 00:12:13,360
Or maybe you need to change a bunch of things before you can actually get there.

132
00:12:14,000 --> 00:12:16,240
So cash availability is important.

133
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:23,120
I have another case of someone from Subclub actually to whom I'm talking where they're fairly on the stage.

134
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:28,240
They've got a bit of a base of organic, but the cash availability they have for paid UAs is fairly limited.

135
00:12:28,560 --> 00:12:31,840
It's in the very low five digits total, not even per month.

136
00:12:32,640 --> 00:12:36,320
And then raise the question of should you actually do any of it?

137
00:12:36,800 --> 00:12:55,360
And some channels can be operated at low scale to some extent, maybe such as, maybe influencer, but most of the bigger platforms that use algorithmic optimization, so that's valid for Meta, TikTok, Google, but that's also valid for all DSP ad networks and so on, don't go there with twenty dollars a day, it's money lost.

138
00:12:55,600 --> 00:13:01,440
Like you're not going to learn anything, but you can't really make the machine work at all, not going to pass threshold.

139
00:13:01,680 --> 00:13:14,560
There's a bunch of like criteria and that minimum bar of the amount you can put to actually start seeing something, whether you like what you see or not is another question, but to actually see anything and learn from it has risen over time.

140
00:13:14,800 --> 00:13:24,480
Ten years ago, it was a lot more manual and there was less events and now it's more and more, let's say, automated and that can work really well in some cases.

141
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:29,360
But if you invest too little, it's doomed to fail, even if your product is fantastic.

142
00:13:29,840 --> 00:13:37,360
With Facebook, I know so much of this is automated with the happy path, you know, where they want you to spend your money is through the algorithmic ad buying.

143
00:13:38,000 --> 00:13:39,200
But are there still ways on Facebook?

144
00:13:39,680 --> 00:13:58,080
So if someone does just have $20 a day to spend, can you manually go in and if you really know who your audience is, actually say, actually set up more manual campaigns and spend those twenty dollars a day with like super highly targeted picking your audiences and stuff or is that just not even possible anymore?

145
00:13:58,640 --> 00:13:59,520
It's technically possible.

146
00:14:00,160 --> 00:14:01,760
I would strongly recommend against it.

147
00:14:02,960 --> 00:14:03,040
Okay.

148
00:14:04,000 --> 00:14:04,800
There are many reasons.

149
00:14:05,200 --> 00:14:10,480
First is the targeting, like people say, is never as narrow, precise as you think it is.

150
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:11,360
So that's one.

151
00:14:12,080 --> 00:14:24,480
Even if you use advanced things like lookalike one percent, like kind of a very narrow audience, but the rest is kind of even if you limit the demographics and the interest and lookalike and maybe a specific area and you reduce.

152
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:27,440
the so there's a bunch of problems that come.

153
00:14:27,840 --> 00:14:30,240
First, when you do that, Facebook tends to penalize you.

154
00:14:30,400 --> 00:14:34,240
If the audience is not big enough, you may not get any delivery at all.

155
00:14:34,480 --> 00:14:35,200
That's a possibility.

156
00:14:35,520 --> 00:14:38,640
And that's most likely, you're going to get it extremely expensive.

157
00:14:38,960 --> 00:14:53,920
Like if you want to go so narrow focus, you're going to pay such a premium to do that that it's probably better to let Facebook figure it out on its own and actually go for these users at a cheaper price by itself.

158
00:14:54,160 --> 00:14:56,480
Like it's probably more efficient.

159
00:14:56,960 --> 00:14:59,920
There's an additional factor or two even.

160
00:15:00,960 --> 00:15:15,360
One is if you're not optimizing like for downstream events, it's most likely you're going to be surf garbage because Facebook and other platforms, they're going to preserve the inventory that is most likely to convert to those who buy for this.

161
00:15:15,680 --> 00:15:20,240
Like when you're saying to Facebook, oh, I'm trying to get free trials, let's say.

162
00:15:20,720 --> 00:15:39,440
What's going to happen in the background is the whole machinery is going to calculate in real time that that particular user who's done this on Facebook, this on Instagram, also interacted with this advertiser before, has converted on a bunch of other apps and seeing that creative, what's the lik probability of that impression converting into actually this event?

163
00:15:40,160 --> 00:15:43,760
And so they preserve this high value inventory for whoever optimized for that.

164
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:47,680
If you run at very low scale, you obviously can't optimize for event.

165
00:15:48,000 --> 00:15:48,800
There's not even enough data.

166
00:15:49,200 --> 00:15:53,200
So you're going to start buying on install or on cliques or even on impression.

167
00:15:53,600 --> 00:15:58,400
What happened in this case is Facebook serves you the inventory that is not going to cover.

168
00:15:58,720 --> 00:16:03,680
And so as you buy all CPI, the install to trial is going to drop.

169
00:16:03,840 --> 00:16:05,760
If you buy on click, people are not going to install.

170
00:16:06,000 --> 00:16:10,160
There's a bunch of people who actually don't install apps on Facebook and Instagram.

171
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:12,800
And obviously if I'm buying on install or on trial.

172
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:17,040
Depending on install or on trials, Facebook is going to show my ads to fewer of these people.

173
00:16:17,600 --> 00:16:22,640
But if you tell them you're after clicks, they'll say, Oh, I've got a bunch of people who are likely to click and do nothing else.

174
00:16:23,040 --> 00:16:24,640
So that's the one I'm going to give you.

175
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:36,080
And so if you're a big brand and you go on full CPM and you say, Okay, my goal is to be seen, I want this campaign to reach eyeballs, I don't need them to take action, Facebook is going to do it.

176
00:16:36,480 --> 00:16:42,000
But they're going to actually serve you people who are never going to do anything because they preserve the other inventory.

177
00:16:42,160 --> 00:16:48,000
So that's the second thing is if you're not optimizing for downstream events, it's likely that the quality of traffic is going to be less.

178
00:16:48,640 --> 00:16:52,800
And the third reason, so there's a premium, you're going to be served to people who don't convert.

179
00:16:53,120 --> 00:16:59,920
And the third one, specific to iOS, but it's going to be the same on Android at some point, is that through the aggregation of data.

180
00:17:00,320 --> 00:17:11,440
So I don't want to be too technical at this point of the podcast, but basically with aggregated data and the sampling of whoever has consented, you're going to be blind basically on who converts or not.

181
00:17:11,840 --> 00:17:18,000
Facebook is not even able to aggregate enough data to even report to you how many install or conversion have happened.

182
00:17:18,320 --> 00:17:22,560
So you're going to hit, let's say, minimum thresholds that Facebook is going to be blind.

183
00:17:22,720 --> 00:17:30,480
You're going to be blind at some point either is it's going to stop delivering completely or you're going to cut it because you say, okay, nothing is coming back or something like that.

184
00:17:30,640 --> 00:17:36,720
