Or why did you look at Latin America that day?

555
00:52:39,680 --> 00:52:43,040
I was like, one, I'm looking at those all the time and you're not and you should.

556
00:52:43,440 --> 00:52:45,040
So that's the first answer.

557
00:52:45,600 --> 00:52:48,560
And second is, if you do it all the time, you start getting a sense for it.

558
00:52:49,120 --> 00:52:56,720
And it's a very exciting point, one because I'm a nerd, but actually from a business sense of view, because it basically enables to improve results faster.

559
00:52:57,040 --> 00:53:14,640
So we've talked about a lot of aspects of what it takes to make paid UA work, but we kind of glossed over one of the most important things is you actually have to like make ads and you actually have to make I was going to say good ads.

560
00:53:14,880 --> 00:53:18,640
I will revise my phrasing and say you have to make performant ads.

561
00:53:19,280 --> 00:53:24,960
Sometimes the most performant ads are actually the worst looking ads, which is flawed.

562
00:53:24,960 --> 00:53:26,160
Which is such a funny quirk to me.

563
00:53:26,560 --> 00:53:28,240
Yeah, let's talk creatives.

564
00:53:28,560 --> 00:53:30,000
Like, can you outsource this?

565
00:53:30,560 --> 00:53:33,680
Is mid journey creatives going to be a thing?

566
00:53:34,480 --> 00:53:43,680
What's that kind of minimal viable creative department to actually think you're going to be able to be successful at ramping up paid UA?

567
00:53:44,160 --> 00:53:48,320
We had to talk about it because, I mean, they're such a massive part of paid UA success.

568
00:53:48,560 --> 00:53:51,520
I remember the early days of it wasn't that important.

569
00:53:51,920 --> 00:53:53,440
I mean, the pressure on ads was less.

570
00:53:53,760 --> 00:53:57,600
People were less used to ads and honestly, we were putting like random stuff that was working.

571
00:53:57,680 --> 00:53:58,320
It was fantastic.

572
00:53:58,560 --> 00:54:02,480
I would put the same ads that I was putting eight years ago, I would go straight to the wall.

573
00:54:02,880 --> 00:54:12,800
It's clear the importance of ads has grown a lot, in particular because a lot of other levers have been removed, as everyone gets more and more automated in a bunch of ways.

574
00:54:13,040 --> 00:54:18,160
And most of the network these days is just, okay, tell me how much money you can afford and what is your goal.

575
00:54:18,800 --> 00:54:23,360
The only thing you have left is what you put as a creative or creative assets.

576
00:54:23,600 --> 00:54:24,240
So this is your lever.

577
00:54:24,560 --> 00:54:26,400
This is how the battle is won or lost.

578
00:54:26,960 --> 00:54:33,920
And I think we should all pretty much have it put in the minimum requirements in the sense of what message you want to put.

579
00:54:34,160 --> 00:54:46,960
And it goes deep inside two things, which is understanding your audience and what triggers them in this, what you have different, what is your USB and so on, which for me is something you have to think before you spend the first dollar.

580
00:54:47,680 --> 00:54:52,080
And then it goes on an iterative mode on what works and keep iterating on it.

581
00:54:52,640 --> 00:55:00,800
One thing that is a bit maddening and makes it very not scientific is that not that it's random, but it's unpredictable.

582
00:55:01,120 --> 00:55:06,320
I used to have a saying which is ugly wins, but the truth is you cannot anticipate what's going to win.

583
00:55:06,480 --> 00:55:16,480
If you show me ten ads and you ask me which one is going to be the most efficient, the probability of me finding the ads is at best the same as randomly picking, okay, let's say that one.

584
00:55:16,560 --> 00:55:24,720
And I realized also for me, it was actually an advantage that I saw a lot of people thinking like, oh, I know this one is going to work better because of this and this and this.

585
00:55:25,280 --> 00:55:29,440
Because I knew from the beginning that I was really bad at anticipating which ads was there.

586
00:55:29,760 --> 00:55:34,800
I had zero like criteria about, I'm going to show them all and may the winner win.

587
00:55:35,040 --> 00:55:35,280
No problem.

588
00:55:35,600 --> 00:55:43,920
I think here the creatives like, they're hard to anticipate, but they're also really hard to interpret why it's winning, which is even more surprising.

589
00:55:44,560 --> 00:55:48,880
But the thing is when a creative starts to win, luckily for us, so it's complicated.

590
00:55:49,440 --> 00:55:50,880
to design and prep.

591
00:55:51,520 --> 00:56:10,480
It's complicated to analyze in terms of why it's been working, but it's not complicated to see what's working because the platform, they're incredibly good at showing the creative that works better, insanely good at with just 100 or 200 impressions, I put 20 ads and they already detected that this one is going to get and it's getting most of the spin.

592
00:56:10,960 --> 00:56:12,000
That's basically the signal.

593
00:56:12,480 --> 00:56:14,400
The creative winner is the one that's getting all the spin.

594
00:56:14,560 --> 00:56:17,040
And 99% of the time, the platform was right.

595
00:56:17,520 --> 00:56:20,720
They do have a couple of false positive and false negative, but that's the exception.

596
00:56:21,040 --> 00:56:26,240
So here, in terms of how you deal with that, one is understandableing your USP and your audience.

597
00:56:26,720 --> 00:56:44,640
One is really understanding the platform, like because the kind of format that can work for a TikTok is completely different at what works in the store in search ad and completely different at what works on Pinterest and on Instagram and on maybe you can recycle a TikTok on reels, but YouTube is going to be another beast.

598
00:56:44,960 --> 00:56:45,840
What is the context of this?

599
00:56:46,160 --> 00:56:47,680
Oh, people are playing that kind of game.

600
00:56:47,840 --> 00:56:48,480
I could put this.

601
00:56:48,800 --> 00:56:59,280
And here comes the problem that a lot of designers and ad producers don't necessarily have the context of where the ad is going to be placed and at which point the user is going to see it.

602
00:56:59,600 --> 00:57:07,120
And that creates a lot of losing ads because this has not been factored, even though it's still hard to plan, but at least like taking a bit of context.

603
00:57:07,680 --> 00:57:08,960
Then it takes a lot of testing.

604
00:57:09,120 --> 00:57:10,960
I mean, finding a winner is really tough.

605
00:57:11,280 --> 00:57:17,280
The thing is when you find a real winner and testing them costs money, but when you find a real winner, it's game changing.

606
00:57:17,440 --> 00:57:18,960
I mean, but like really game changing.

607
00:57:19,120 --> 00:57:24,800
I can remember a couple of times in history where we find like a it's not a ten x winner, it's a hundred x winner.

608
00:57:24,960 --> 00:57:26,960
It can change the course of the company to find one.

609
00:57:27,200 --> 00:57:34,800
Like it's vastly understated how you could completely change the course of a company just by finding this winner.

610
00:57:35,040 --> 00:57:36,240
But for this, it means you deploy.

611
00:57:36,640 --> 00:57:40,000
And it's a bit of a number game of you have to test a lot of things.

612
00:57:40,560 --> 00:57:46,320
One of the most common mistakes in creative is people are testing twenty variants of the same concept.

613
00:57:46,480 --> 00:57:49,360
Like they're testing over and over the same thing with small variants.

614
00:57:49,760 --> 00:57:51,360
You have to test radically different things.

615
00:57:51,760 --> 00:57:53,440
Is it a person or is it an animation?

616
00:57:53,840 --> 00:57:55,840
Is it provocative or is it really bland?

617
00:57:56,000 --> 00:57:56,800
Is it strong color?

618
00:57:57,360 --> 00:57:58,160
Is there a lot of text?

619
00:57:58,720 --> 00:58:03,760
Like there's so many elements that you can change that it's not even the message you're communicating, but the format.

620
00:58:04,160 --> 00:58:06,400
Like, let's take a longer video, a shorter video.

621
00:58:06,800 --> 00:58:10,400
Do we start with the app or do we start with a person or do we start with whatever?

622
00:58:10,640 --> 00:58:12,560
And you have to be really radical.

623
00:58:13,120 --> 00:58:19,520
For me, the framework is really, we're going to test a lot of different concepts, even if they're not completely finished and polished and nice.

624
00:58:20,160 --> 00:58:24,800
Wherever we get a bit of traction, this is where we're going to add layers of, let's do variants of this.

625
00:58:25,120 --> 00:58:26,560
Like there is a bit of traction there.

626
00:58:26,880 --> 00:58:28,320
Let's dig, let's drill down.

627
00:58:28,480 --> 00:58:33,920
And okay, let's test in another order, let's test longer, let's test with another person doing exactly the same.

628
00:58:34,480 --> 00:58:39,280
Let's take the same scene or let's change elements after elements until we polish this winner.

629
00:58:40,000 --> 00:58:51,600
And one thing you said is, at this sentence, there's ugly wins that sometimes like the ads that are most performing, they're not even completely off brand guidelines.

630
00:58:51,840 --> 00:59:27,600
I had a ton of problems with other teams saying this is what you're showing to users like this is detrimental to the company in the long term like this is problematic and say yeah but it's bringing twice as much of the money so no brainer I'm going to keep showing it is actually finding ways of okay here I've got a winner how can I realign it a little bit with I know this concept is winning but also it might have consequences in the long term that I'm not seeing because UA is very short termist so not only try to find variance and the elements that work but actually also realign it with the message that the company wants to give.

631
00:59:28,160 --> 00:59:29,360
That's really a complex topic.

632
00:59:29,600 --> 00:59:35,680
I personally predicted two or three years ago that we would start seeing the role of creative manager, I call it.

633
00:59:35,920 --> 00:59:37,440
There's not really any name for it.

634
00:59:37,840 --> 00:59:42,320
And I'm surprised to see that it's not more standardized because the need for this is massive.

635
00:59:42,800 --> 00:59:45,920
Everybody's doing it, but it's completely unstructured.

636
00:59:46,320 --> 00:59:49,600
And you ask also a little bit about externalization and so on.

637
00:59:50,000 --> 00:59:52,880
Personally, when I get this question, I'm like, I want both.

638
00:59:53,440 --> 00:59:55,280
I want to have a designer in house.

639
00:59:55,920 --> 00:59:58,000
I want to call creators.

640
00:59:58,400 --> 00:59:59,680
I want to use agency.

641
01:00:00,160 --> 01:00:06,080
to produce ads and I want you to try not shoot a video selfie on your own and let's see if it works.

642
01:00:06,240 --> 01:00:15,440
I know a bunch of companies where the best performing ads is actually the CEO shooting himself in a very poor environment, like old phone, whatever, it's blurry, whatnot.

643
01:00:15,840 --> 01:00:16,480
That often works.

644
01:00:16,800 --> 01:00:24,480
And the idea behind using different ways of production is that everybody will come with extremely different concepts and styles and so on.

645
01:00:24,720 --> 01:00:26,240
And that's the only way to find winners.

646
01:00:26,720 --> 01:00:37,680
If you always rely on the same person to create ads, no matter how good this person is, you're going to hit a ceiling because we're all limited in our creativity range, let's say.

647
01:00:38,000 --> 01:00:39,680
So I basically want different people to do this.

648
01:00:40,400 --> 01:00:56,160
And this is why over the course of the last two, three years, not just because influencer marketing was growing, that advertisers use creators more and more not to reach their audience, but to actually use their ability to understand the channel to produce content.

649
01:00:56,720 --> 01:01:06,320
When we talk about user generated content like UGC, the ads that are created by influencers mostly, it's actually creator content because they know the role of the platform.

650
01:01:06,960 --> 01:01:10,160
They're really good at making these videos that don't look like ads as well.

651
01:01:10,320 --> 01:01:11,520
So people are going to skip less.

652
01:01:11,920 --> 01:01:21,920
And on paid social at least, it may not be true for every channel, but it's clear that content created by creators is what has been winning in the last one or two years.

653
01:01:22,320 --> 01:01:24,400
That's what the younger generation wants to see.

654
01:01:24,800 --> 01:01:30,160
And it's not the only way to do ads, but if you're not doing any of it, you're missing out on something huge.

655
01:01:30,960 --> 01:01:32,560
That is fascinating.

656
01:01:32,960 --> 01:01:41,120
I haven't heard anyone quite go as deep into the reasons and the variety of things that need to be taken into account.

657
01:01:41,280 --> 01:01:45,840
I think there's so many people in the industry still thinking, we need to figure this out in-house.

658
01:01:46,000 --> 01:01:48,800
Like our designer needs to be the one making the ads.

659
01:01:49,600 --> 01:01:57,440
And it's fascinating to understand why you should not try and build intuition because.

660
01:01:57,680 --> 01:02:05,520
Because if you think you're building intuition, you're handicapping yourself because you're not going to do as broad of experimentation.

661
01:02:05,760 --> 01:02:09,600
You're going to say that ads are not going to work and then it would.

662
01:02:10,240 --> 01:02:13,520
The biggest takeaway for me is just like, don't rely on intuition.

663
01:02:13,680 --> 01:02:15,680
Don't try and make ads you think are going to work.

664
01:02:16,000 --> 01:02:17,360
Try and make ads that are ads.

665
01:02:17,920 --> 01:02:18,720
There's an added argument.

666
01:02:19,040 --> 01:02:22,800
I think this is interesting here to add a bit of the closure on creative.

667
01:02:23,440 --> 01:02:28,240
But there's another element which is really the creative is the targeting.

668
01:02:28,480 --> 01:02:31,920
Like most platforms, you don't target people at all.

669
01:02:32,480 --> 01:02:35,760
Like on Facebook, you can, but the most efficient strategy is just go broad.

670
01:02:36,000 --> 01:02:41,120
You just put the country and I want people to be at least 18 all of the US and have an iPhone.

671
01:02:41,600 --> 01:02:45,760
And then the creative and the events that you're inputing are doing the targeting work.

672
01:02:46,080 --> 01:02:55,280
And if you only have one team, whether it's in a house or even an external, they're going to gear towards one concept that is maybe attractive.

673
01:02:55,520 --> 01:03:21,440
And you might find one winner this way or a couple, but you want also the diversity in concept because ads are going to work for different audience and you want them at the same time in the account because Facebook is, oh, that ads resonate to that kind of user and that ads resonate to that kind of user and you can group them differently and actually expand your audience and find performance in more groups that you would have found otherwise.

674
01:03:21,840 --> 01:03:28,400
If you always keep milking the same winner over and over again, you're also missing out on a ton of potential audience for your app.

675
01:03:28,800 --> 01:03:30,160
And so you have to go broad.

676
01:03:30,560 --> 01:03:35,600
No matter how niche you are, there are always a ton of triggers to different people.

677
01:03:36,400 --> 01:03:39,280
And those, they're not demographic criteria or interests.

678
01:03:39,760 --> 01:03:43,040
They're really what you're saying about your app and how you're saying it in the format.

679
01:03:43,360 --> 01:03:47,280
So that's also why you want to never give all the creative to one person.

680
01:03:47,680 --> 01:03:56,000
I don't care whether it's in house, even the creators, you want to test a lot of creative because if all your creative is done by one creator, you run into the same problem again.

681
01:03:56,800 --> 01:03:56,880
Yeah.

682
01:03:57,760 --> 01:04:09,600
So the one thing you did mention that is a bit of intuition and maybe something that should have some level of focus in the iCreative is the unique selling prop.

683
01:04:10,560 --> 01:04:14,880
How do you incorporate that into this variety of testing?

684
01:04:15,200 --> 01:04:22,160
Like in your mind, does an ad need to explicitly say something about the app bringing value?

685
01:04:22,480 --> 01:04:24,160
Does it need to say something specific?

686
01:04:24,800 --> 01:04:28,160
But then each app has like multiple layers of unique selling propositions, right?

687
01:04:28,400 --> 01:04:29,920
Like my weather app, I always think about this.

688
01:04:30,080 --> 01:04:32,160
Like some people just like to talk about the weather.

689
01:04:32,320 --> 01:04:33,280
Some people care about whether they.

690
01:04:33,680 --> 01:04:36,080
they need an umbrella to work today because they're walking.

691
01:04:36,400 --> 01:04:41,120
Some people, it's like there's so many different kinds of features that would resonate with different audiences.

692
01:04:41,600 --> 01:04:48,080
So it's not that you pick one unique selling prop, one unique feature, and like all your creatives are just variations on that.

693
01:04:48,320 --> 01:04:52,000
But how do you think about like ultimately you're still selling something?

694
01:04:52,320 --> 01:05:00,320
Is there some like overarching principle that you think about that an ad needs at a minimum communicate something?

695
01:05:01,280 --> 01:05:02,240
The simple answer is no.

696
01:05:02,560 --> 01:05:10,720
Like there's no clear pattern of this kind of USB is definitely most likely to work or like this kind of expressing the USB.

697
01:05:11,360 --> 01:05:17,680
There are some verticals where I insist on this because you have to differentiate in markets that are more competitive.

698
01:05:18,000 --> 01:05:28,960
And so it depends on the phase you're in, but one typical example would be meditation apps where you could go five years ago with a completely bland message about, oh, we're doing meditation.

699
01:05:29,200 --> 01:05:29,920
It might have worked.

700
01:05:30,160 --> 01:05:33,840
But a lot of people have tested them and a lot of products have grown.

701
01:05:34,080 --> 01:05:37,760
And now I'm seeing founders come to me and say, yeah, I'd like to launch this meditation app.

702
01:05:38,000 --> 01:05:38,720
Can you help me grow it?

703
01:05:38,880 --> 01:05:40,080
I say, okay, what's the twist?

704
01:05:40,240 --> 01:05:41,680
Are you a meditation app for seniors?

705
01:05:41,760 --> 01:05:43,120
Are you a meditation app for kids?

706
01:05:43,280 --> 01:05:47,840
Are you a meditation app that is inspired by whatever philosophy behind?

707
01:05:48,000 --> 01:05:49,040
Or what's your angle?

708
01:05:49,280 --> 01:05:50,320
What are you doing different from the other?

709
01:05:50,480 --> 01:05:51,200
I say, oh no.

710
01:05:51,200 --> 01:05:53,200
Oh no, I just have great content and run meditation.

711
01:05:54,080 --> 01:06:01,520
So you just have the exact same stuff that the other 500 meditation apps that have been running at billions of dollars for the last five years.

712
01:06:01,840 --> 01:06:04,400
And I said, yes, okay, we can't work together.

713
01:06:04,640 --> 01:06:08,880
So I think in those verticals that are extremely competitive, you have to differentiate with something.

714
01:06:09,600 --> 01:06:13,520
But it doesn't mean that your twist is what's going to work in the ads, not necessarily.

715
01:06:14,000 --> 01:06:15,280
Sometimes the USP comes after.

716
01:06:15,760 --> 01:06:19,920
Sometimes the creative is just a teasing that is not expressing what makes you different.

717
01:06:20,240 --> 01:06:21,040
You're not saying it there.

718
01:06:21,280 --> 01:06:23,920
You're going to say it later in the app store or even in the product.

719
01:06:24,640 --> 01:06:40,720
Maybe you can find success with a creative for your weather app that is just showing a funny situation with umbrella of people losing their umbrella and then the transition is well done and people discover later that your app is better for weather because of XYZ.

720
01:06:41,120 --> 01:06:50,640
Like it doesn't mean that the USP needs to be in the creative, but I think you need to know your USP to test stuff towards users, see what makes them react.

721
01:06:50,880 --> 01:06:52,560
Like it leads you to their interest.

722
01:06:53,040 --> 01:06:55,520
And very often the best ads, they are a bit indirect.

723
01:06:55,840 --> 01:06:58,640
They're not about, oh, my app is doing this, you want to download it.

724
01:06:58,880 --> 01:06:59,280
Never worked.

725
01:06:59,680 --> 01:07:04,960
But say, okay, people who are interested in that, they very often talk about the rain.

726
01:07:05,520 --> 01:07:07,600
Find funny stuff about the rain, put it on TikTok.

727
01:07:07,920 --> 01:07:10,960
Find hard facts about the rain and put them on Reddit or Twitter.

728
01:07:11,440 --> 01:07:13,760
Fine, you know, oh, that's what they're interested about.

729
01:07:14,080 --> 01:07:15,120
This is going to be my hook.

730
01:07:15,440 --> 01:07:18,560
The hook is like the first second of three seconds of your ad.

731
01:07:18,880 --> 01:07:23,600
It is extremely important because if people don't stop in their scrolling, you're dead already.

732
01:07:24,240 --> 01:07:25,520
You need them to stop on your ads.

733
01:07:25,840 --> 01:07:26,640
This is fundamental.

734
01:07:27,200 --> 01:07:32,080
And very often, if you talk about what you're doing, there's no way people are going to stop on your ad.

735
01:07:32,400 --> 01:07:42,400
So you need to find kind of a hook and then slowly transition and maybe it's not even in the creative transition into what you're doing actually to solve that problem or to do it differently.

736
01:07:42,800 --> 01:07:48,080
And it's kind of tricky because sometimes what the user wants to see is not what you're doing and finding this way.

737
01:07:48,640 --> 01:07:51,040
of transitioning is not easy at all.

738
01:07:51,280 --> 01:07:53,440
So I don't think the USP needs to be the creative.

739
01:07:53,520 --> 01:07:54,640
And I don't think there is a pattern.

740
01:07:54,800 --> 01:07:56,960
And I don't think there is a particular recipe.

741
01:07:57,520 --> 01:08:00,400
I've seen creative that are so different work for different people.

742
01:08:00,720 --> 01:08:02,160
It's amazing to watch.

743
01:08:02,480 --> 01:08:08,560
Like, I'm friend with the developer of the motivation app and developers called Monkit Apps, very successful apps.

744
01:08:09,280 --> 01:08:14,400
And his best performing apps of all time, there was a screenshot of a notification.

745
01:08:14,960 --> 01:08:23,840
And I've tried this trick with other apps from not the same exact USP, but let's say approaching USP, never managed to make it work.

746
01:08:24,080 --> 01:08:29,520
Like, sometimes it's the time, maybe you had something really different in it there or maybe it was the first or maybe what?

747
01:08:29,920 --> 01:08:37,360
And obviously you could see in the notification it's an app but it wasn't particularly saying how the app was better or different and that ad just worked.

748
01:08:37,520 --> 01:08:44,240
Like sometimes it's surprising to see what works but it's also amazing to see that different things work for different apps.

749
01:08:44,560 --> 01:08:49,600
So have to try it up like literally it's hard to do any other way than trial and error.

750
01:08:50,080 --> 01:09:00,800
